 - Tests: `node test/run-tests.js` (mocked axios, real HTTP). The files MUST live in test/ (ROOT is the parent folder). The harness backs up, blanks and restores models.json, usage-state.json AND provider-limits.json - usage-state holds the persisted Google windows and an old run polluted the next. QP_WAIT_NOTICE_MS exists only so tests don't wait 10s.
 
+NOTES FOR FUTURE ME (added 2026-11-26) - read before touching timeouts, waiting or Literouter
+- TWO UNRELATED 5-MINUTE PROBLEMS. Do not mix them up (I did once, in this file).
+  (A) Long THINKING, months ago. GLM-5.3 (or Kimi 3, I forget which) on NVIDIA with thinking at MAX and no way to lower or turn it off thought for 600+ seconds, easily. One site I used the proxy on had a HIDDEN 5-minute cut-off on thinking that I did not know about; it counts from the FIRST token, so the wait before the first token can be as long as it likes. On another site without that cut-off the model just spiralled for 600+ s. At first I blamed the token limit; it never reached it, and that was ruled out for certain. So (A) was that site's limit (plus a model that cannot be told to think less), not Q-Proxy and not NVIDIA.
+  (B) Waiting to even START, 2026-11-26. On a very slow NVIDIA day, if no output at all (not one token) arrives within 5 minutes, NVIDIA itself gives up with HTTP 504. Seen in the Admin log: "still waiting for nvidia/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash to start answering - 5m 0s and no output yet (queue/connect time, not thinking)" and two seconds later "GATEWAY_TIMEOUT nvidia/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash attempt 1/100 failed (GATEWAY_TIMEOUT - HTTP 504) - retrying in 250ms". How I got there: I first thought the NEWEST version (a57f15e, "Limits, waiting and defaults") caused it, so I reverted it (09e21e3). Then I confirmed the OLD version had the same 504, so the commit was never the cause (it was reverted for nothing; that is why the zip can re-apply it). Then I took it for a rehash of (A) because I had forgotten the specifics, until the log above showed it is a different problem: (A) is a site's cut-off AFTER the first token, (B) is NVIDIA's own cut-off BEFORE any token. A longer Q-Proxy wait cannot help (B); the 504 retry (NVIDIA hops retry up to 100 times) is the right response.
+- The zip I sent on 2026-11-26 (q-proxy-update-v4) re-applies most of that reverted commit (defaults.json, limit basis, finishAfterDisconnect, hop-behavior, admin-key hardening) plus extra Admin pages. It is NOT merged. Do not call anything in it "missing" from the repo: the repo is behind on purpose.
+- GOOGLE LIMITS re-pasted 2026-11-26 from 4 screenshots (provider-limits.json google, capturedAt 2026-11-26). New rows: Genie 4, Gemini 4 Neon / Hydrogen / Carbon / Argon, Gemini Decision 1 (Decisions API, 200 RPM / 50K TPM / 10K RPD), Gemini Omni 1.2 Flash, Gemini Nano Banana 2.1, Lyria Realtime (15 RPM / - / 1.5K RPD). Only Gemini 4 Hydrogen has free chat limits (5 / 250K / 20). Not seen in the screenshots, kept on purpose: gemini-3.7-flash (a live hop), gemini-omni-flash, veo-3-*. A gap between screenshots 1 and 2 may hide them; confirm before deleting.
+- LITEROUTER CONTEXT. Free (":free", 0x cost) models are a FIXED 5,000 tokens whatever you set. Base models (no suffix, or ":metered") also default to 5,000 and the dashboard's Chat memory sliders can raise it; by hand I see non-free models stop at 15,000, but docs.literouter.com/chat-optimization publishes no per-plan maximum (its only ceilings are Claude's separate memory: 5,000 on Basic/Standard, 25,000 Plus, 50,000 Pro and above). Those docs describe NO API, header or request parameter for the sliders: dashboard only, as far as they say. Not affected: ":full-context", ":metered:full-context", ":payg", ":32k-context" to ":256k-context". What "summarized" means: a longer chat is condensed or left out by Literouter, never rejected. I decided I do not care how. The proxy only WARNS: server.js literouterContextCap() logs [context] for free ids (5,000, fixed) and for base ids (provider-limits.json literouter.baseContextTokens, default 5,000; edit it after raising the sliders, Q-Proxy cannot read them).
+- LITEROUTER /v1/models: their docs (docs.literouter.com/endpoints) say each model has minimum_tier, model_cost and modality, in the OpenAI /models shape. Nobody here reads those yet (the sync still uses the hand-typed table), so it is the first thing to try if I want live tier/cost instead of pasting. Not verified against a real response.
+- LITEROUTER MODEL CARDS 2026-11-26 (provider-limits.json): deepseek-v4.1-flash:free and -cheap:free and minimax-m3.1-flash:free = 100/day on Basic, deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017:free = unlimited on Basic; the base versions cost 1x, qwen3.6-27b 1.4x. ":32k-context" variants of those (1.4x-1.8x flat) need the Standard plan: unusable, listed under literouter.standardOnly.
+- CROWLLM (crowllm.com): added as provider "crowllm" (model suffix -cr, env CROWLLM_API_KEY, base https://crowllm.com/v1, one key from https://crowllm.com/keys, Discord sign-in, no card). GET https://crowllm.com/v1/models needs no key (a third party counted 232 ids); GET https://crowllm.com/api/pricing lists billing per model. Its author counts ~70 free of 153 chat models; ids ending ":free" bill $0, but about 14 ":free" ids still bill credit, so the sync only labels ":free" as "free", check /api/pricing before trusting one. Rate limits per minute/day are NOT published anywhere I could read, so rpm/rpd are blank (let a 429 teach me). I did not find the "slow way to earn tokens" on any page I could read (docs, pricing and models pages came back empty); the facts above come from CrowLLM's own integration pull requests (litellm #48022, awesome-freellm-apis #52), written by the person who runs it, not from a docs page, and tools/streaming support is unverified. Untested against the live service.
+- OPENCODE-STYLE MODEL INFO. OpenCode shows "200K context / Text, Image, Tools" for a model because its config says so; Q-Proxy models showed nothing because /v1/models had no such fields. Now: any models.json entry may carry "info": {context, maxOutput, input:[..], output:[..], tools, reasoning}. modelInfoFor() fills what it can FROM FACTS (tools from the hop's "tools" flag, reasoning from its schema, context for Literouter hops) and leaves the rest empty; it never guesses a context or a modality. /v1/models now adds context_length, max_output_tokens, architecture.input_modalities, supported_parameters. GET /opencode.json (same auth as /v1/models) returns a paste-ready OpenCode provider block (limit.context/output, modalities, tool_call, reasoning; key via {env:QPROXY_KEY}). The schema is written from memory of OpenCode's custom-provider config: check it against my OpenCode version. Most models still have NO context/modalities until I fill "info" (no Admin form for it yet; POST /admin/api/models accepts and validates it). Sync-add does not pre-fill it yet.
+
 CONFUSING BUT NOT BUGS
 - Measured: the same 503 gets 1 try on Google and up to 100 on NIM (~6.4 min of 250ms->4s backoff); a plain 429 is retried like a 503 and ignores Retry-After. Accepted for now.
-- Time to first output on GLM-5.3 can be minutes (seen 4m14s). It is queue/connect time, not thinking; the Admin log prints a waiting notice after 10s and "first output arrived after ...".
+- Time to first output on GLM-5.3 can be minutes (seen 4m14s). It is queue/connect time, not thinking (on a very slow day NVIDIA itself answers 504 after 5 minutes with no output - see "TWO UNRELATED 5-MINUTE PROBLEMS" below); the Admin log prints a waiting notice after 10s and "first output arrived after ...".
 - Clocks: every console stamp is UTC (boot prints the server clock); the Admin panel shows my local time (GMT+1); Render's own dashboard prefix is a third, unexplained clock - trust the stamp inside the line.
 - Google limits are per PROJECT. I run ONE Google key and won't get more accounts (ban risk). Extra keys from the same project add no quota, but the proxy tracks each key's window separately and would count them as extra capacity. Never suggest more Google accounts.
 - Google 429 learning (Admin banner "limits may be outdated -> Refresh limits") corrects a hop's value from the 429's quotaId/quotaValue. It can only learn when a request gets past our own limits and Google rejects it.
